A hypothesis test asks: "is this data surprising under the null hypothesis H₀?" The p-value is P(data this extreme | H₀) — NOT P(H₀ is true). Reject H₀ when p < α (often 0.05). A 95% confidence interval is a procedure that covers the true parameter in 95% of repetitions — not "95% chance the truth is inside this interval". Worked example (verified): conversion rate baseline 10% (H₀: p=0.10). In n=400 trials we observe 52 conversions (ς=0.13). SE = √(0.1×0.9/400) = √(0.000225) = 0.015. z = (0.13−0.10)/0.015 = 2.0. Two-sided p ≈ 0.0455 < 0.05 — reject H₀; the lift is statistically significant. But "significant" ≠ important: a 3-point lift may not cover the feature’s cost. Report effect size and confidence interval alongside every p-value, pre-register the hypothesis (no p-hacking), and remember that with 20 metrics tested, one will be "significant" by chance alone.

