A vector v in R^n is an n-tuple; add componentwise, scale by multiplying each component. The dot product u·v = Σuᵢvᵢ measures alignment: positive when pointing the same way, zero when orthogonal, negative when opposed. Worked example (verified): u=(1,2,3), v=(4,−1,2). u+v = (5,1,5). 2u = (2,4,6). u·v = 1·4 + 2×(−1) + 3×2 = 4−2+6 = 8. Since 8 > 0, the angle between them is acute. In embeddings and attention, dot products ARE similarity scores: softmax(QK^T) is a matrix of scaled dot products deciding what each token attends to. Orthogonal vectors (dot 0) represent unrelated directions — the geometric meaning of "uncorrelated features".

