A proposition is a statement that is either true (T) or false (F) — never both. The connectives build compounds: negation ¬p, conjunction p∧q (and), disjunction p∨q (or, inclusive), implication p→q (if p then q), biconditional p↔q (p iff q). A truth table enumerates every input combination: n variables need 2^n rows. Worked example: build the table for p→q. Recall p→q is F only when p=T and q=F. Rows: (T,T)→T; (T,F)→F; (F,T)→T; (F,F)→T. So "if 2+2=5 then the moon is cheese" is TRUE as a material implication — the antecedent is false. This surprises beginners, but implication promises only that a true antecedent never yields a false consequent. In policy engines and code (if-statements), this is exactly the semantics: the rule fires only when the condition holds; a false condition makes the implication vacuously true.

