To prove P(n) for all n ≥ n₀: (1) base case P(n₀); (2) inductive step: assume P(k) (induction hypothesis), prove P(k+1). Worked example (verified): prove 1+2+⋯+n = n(n+1)/2. Base n=1: LHS 1, RHS 1·2/2 = 1 ✓. Assume true for k. For k+1: LHS = k(k+1)/2 + (k+1) = (k+1)(k/2+1) = (k+1)(k+2)/2 ✓ — exactly the formula at n=k+1. Numeric spot-check: n=5 gives 1+2+3+4+5=15 and 5·6/2=15 ✓; n=100 gives 5050 = 100×101/2 ✓. Induction is the proof method behind every loop-correctness argument and every recursive algorithm analysis: the base case is the loop’s first iteration, the step is "if the invariant holds entering iteration k, it holds leaving it".

